===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*wurdi}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*wurdiz}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*wr̥ti-}}, a verbal abstract from the root {{m|ine-pro|*wert-||to turn}} .

Related to {{cog|la|vertere}}, the Old English verb {{m|ang|weorþan||to grow into, become}}, {{cog|nl|worden}}, {{cog|de|werden}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|wurd}}, {{cog|goh|wurt}}, {{cog|non|urðr||fate}} ({{cog|non|Urðr||one of 3 norns}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|wyrd}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|f}}

# [[fate]], [[destiny]]
#* ''{{w|Beowulf}}'', [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Beowulf_(Harrison_and_Sharp) line 455b]
#*: {{quote|ang|Gǣð ā '''wyrd''' swā hīo sċel.|'''Fate''' always goes as it must.}}
# {{lb|ang|in the plural}} the [[Fates]]
# [[event]], [[occurrence]]
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://archive.org/details/kingalfredsangl00boetgoog/page/n134/mode/2up translation] of [[w:Boethius|Boethius']] ''{{w|Metres of Boethius}}, lines 226-238''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ne þearf lēoda nān wēnan þǣre '''wyrde''', þæt weriġe flǣsc þæt mōd monna ǣniġes, eallunga tō him æfter mæġ onwendan; ac þā unþēawas ǣlces mōdes, and þ inġeþonc ǣlces monnes þone līchoman līt þider hit wille.''|Nor needs any man expect that '''event''', that the vile flesh the mind of any man, Altogether to it should ever turn; but the vices of every mind, and the thought of every man, leads the body Whither it will.}}

